(module crux-validate-subject-hardcoded)
;; THE VALIDATOR driver-spec.txt section 7:288-291 warns about, written out:
;; "a validator that hardcodes the values seen in one run will silently report
;; emptiness on the next."
;;
;; The body accepts exactly one size above the floor, 1638, which is the byte
;; length of experiments/rfc-swarm/prompts/stage-B-scope.md in this tree. A
;; validator fitted to one committed run looks like this: it passes every
;; regression the run it was fitted to produces and rejects the next subject
;; entirely. Proposal section 6.1 calls this "the item most likely to be lost",
;; and a comment is not a check.
;;
;; THE GUARD `(> size floor)` IS WHAT MAKES THIS A DISCRIMINATING MUTANT, and
;; it was measured, not assumed. Without it the body ALSO refutes [V7-FLOOR],
;; because 1638 sits below some floors and the unguarded body would pass an
;; undersized output whenever the floor exceeded 1638; the mutant would then
;; have refuted through a post that has nothing to do with subject hardcoding.
;; Measured at v0.14.86 over this body:
;;
;;   [V7-PRESENCE] + [V7-FLOOR] alone   -> SAFE
;;   [V7-NO-HARDCODE] alone             -> REFUTED
;;   [V7-NO-FLOOR] alone                -> REFUTED
;;
;; [V7-NO-FLOOR] is a NAMED CONSEQUENCE of [V7-NO-HARDCODE] rather than an
;; independent obligation -- a negative floor is below every non-negative size
;; -- so no body can break one and keep the other. It is stated separately in
;; validate.llmll because it is the stage-I disposition of proposal section 9.1
;; item 2 and a reader should be able to find it by name.
(type Verdict (| Absent) (| Malformed) (| Passed))
(def verdict-of [present: bool size: int floor: int] -> Verdict
  (pre (>= size 0))
  (post (=> (not present) (= result Absent)))
  (post (=> (and present (<= size floor)) (= result Malformed)))
  (post (=> (and present (> size floor)) (= result Passed)))
  (post (=> (and present (< floor 0)) (= result Passed)))
  (if present
      (if (and (> size floor) (= size 1638)) Passed Malformed)
      Absent))
